hi there Steve Kaufman here again  talking about language learning as usual  if you enjoy these videos please  subscribe to my channel today I want to  talk about meaningful input and how it  applies to my Greek learning okay  stephen krashen speaks of meaningful  input comprehensible input that  acquiring these meaningful messages  compelling input is basically how we  acquire languages how we get languages  into our brain so that we can then  subsequently produce them so for me  greek presents certain problems that i  haven't experienced in a while the when  i started on russian which is well over  it's got to be over ten years ago  russian then was the first sort of  language where most of the vocabulary  was unrelated to vocabulary that i had  from other languages  leaving aside chinese and japanese but  I'm saying you know German with English  English with French English with Spanish  there's a lot of vocabulary that you get  free when you get into Russian the  number of words that are identifiable is  much much less but then after doing  Russian then I did you know check  Ukrainian polish when I did Romanian of  course there's a lot of vocabulary that  that is similar to Italian but Greek  it's yeah there's some words you know  hora knows four time or homelesses color  photos light que photo there's some of  that but overwhelmingly the vocabulary  is new you got to learn it so it's  tougher for me then what I have been  experiencing over the last ten years or  so so I have we have our sort of  beginner stuff at link and then I got  onto these mini stories translating from  these English many stories and that was  a boon for me and I'm up to 40 of them  but we are short now of the the person  who's producing these in English seems  to have slowed down so before I get  further any further here if any of you  out there now we have all kinds of  volunteers translating these many  stories into 30 plus languages Arabic  Hebrew  I don't know Latvian and this is going  to be in public domain and so forth and  so on and I find these stories  tremendous from my Greek now I'm stuck  I've only I'm up to 40 so if any of you  in addition to those of you who have  volunteered to translate if you want to  create some stories all right then  following this formula the three-part  formula with lots of repetition using  largely high frequency verbs nouns don't  matter so much but verbs and we have  five levels so we now have level one  which is basically word order positive  negative question level two is the  different tenses level three we  introduced a concept such a purpose in  10th why because given the fact that  verbs of motion verbs of giving and  taking more work on tenses that's level  3 level 4 is probability necessity  possibility maybe doubt conditional  whenever those kinds of things and level  5 is where you want to express your  opinion in my view on the other hand it  would appear bla bla bla and more tenses  so there's five levels so that at each  level you can focus on practicing  patterns because don't forget this is  primarily about gaining initially some  familiarity with the patterns how  different languages express different  things and also as a place that you can  go back to to practice these patterns  and along the way you pick up some  vocabulary but they are vocabulary heavy  so for me and my Greek for example if I  look at a new lesson in our mini story  series there might only be 20% new words  whereas if I pull a newspaper article in  Greek there's 55% new words and it's  just hard it's hard especially because  there's no freebies if I'm into you know  polish from Ukranian 55% new words but a  lot of those words I'll know from  another Slavic language so I'm hoping  maybe again if some of you would  volunteer to write  so many stories for us in your own  language whatever that language is we'll  use it as learning material for that  language plus we would also like to get  the English translation so we could  translate it into other languages so we  start to accumulate this again in the  public domain collection of stories and  the advantage of having people write  these stories in their various native  languages is that we aren't too  culturally you know bound to essentially  in an english-speaking kind of cultural  environment so if any of you want to  write stories for us please let me know  here in the comment section or send me  an email at Steve at link ili ngq calm  and we'll get you in touch with zoar n  so we can get some more stories going in  this series but getting back to this  meaningful content doesn't so here I am  I right I find it too difficult to to  deal with 50 plus percent new words I  found a graded reader supposedly  folktales in Greek but I'm not that  interested in folktales it's okay and it  focus have a lot of vocabulary you know  the Fox  you know the crow stuff that's not  necessarily relevant to me one of the  best graded readers I ever had was in  Chinese 20 lectures on Chinese culture  which used use sort of a limited range  of vocabulary to talk about Chinese  history and Chinese culture  which is an interest of mine and  particularly in learning a new language  learning about a stirring the cultures  is very interesting so graded readers  are great I know  Oh Lee Richards is working on graded  readers for Spanish and so forth and of  course I want to find these wherever  they are  I'll buy the e-book put it through  caliber convert it into a format that we  could use a link imported into Lee and  start learning with it but having this  meaningful content is so important  because to step from these many stories  right up to the newspaper is too  difficult I think if I get to a hundred  minutes  Dory's I'll be a little further along  and nothing I wanted to mention - on  meaningful content we have the series at  link called who is she which is kind of  where I started with Greek and it was  very difficult for me very difficult but  now having done 40 of these many stories  I go back to who is she and it's a  delight it's a delight not only to see  words that you now know but also to  recognize the patterns I mean you're  going blind initially even though you  read an explanation of how they deal  with you know future whatever it is it  just flies by you but now that you've  experienced it through these stories  especially are many stories with all the  repetition and you have a sense of what  that means on how that pattern works and  then I go back to who is she and I see  those patterns and now it's just the  second nature common commonplace for me  it's it's it's a delight it's always a  delight to go back to some of the  material that you started with and now  see that in fact it's no longer  difficult for you anyway so just a  little bit of a discussion on the  importance of meaning comprehensible  input how we get that meaningful  comprehensible input  I believe this mini story idea which  started with Blaine Ray was then picked  up by AJ hole picked up by picked up by  filter in real polish PL which is where  I first discovered it and now I want to  make this available in as many languages  as possible with the help of volunteers  who either help us translate stories or  possibly there are people out there who  are creative and are willing to try to  create these stories in their own  language and also provide us with an  English translation so we could include  these and if you are willing to do that  then let me know and I'll send you this  description of the five levels and yeah  maybe we can move the project forward so  thank you for listening  [Music]

